MASTER BUILD PROMPT — CREATOR STUDIO PRO
Professional Video Editing Website + Windows Desktop Application
Act as a senior software architect, video editing engineer, UI/UX designer, and QA engineer.

Build a production-oriented video editing application named Creator Studio Pro with a professional multi-track video editor, local-only project storage, Kannada and English language support, optional AI creator tools, and a Windows desktop application.

Do not build only a landing page or a visual prototype. Build a working application with real functionality, clear error handling, and automated tests.
PHASE 1 — PROJECT ARCHITECTURE
Use:
- React and TypeScript for the user interface.
- Vite for frontend development.
- Electron for the Windows desktop application.
- SQLite for local project metadata and settings in desktop mode.
- FFmpeg/FFmpeg.wasm or another suitable rendering engine for video processing.
- IndexedDB and the browser File System Access API where supported for browser-based local workflows.
- Vitest for unit tests and Playwright for UI and end-to-end tests.
Use a maintainable modular architecture. Separate the editor UI, timeline state, media management, project serialization, rendering, storage adapters, and AI integrations.

Create two application targets:
1. Browser-based website.
2. Windows desktop application.
Reuse the same editor UI and business logic where possible.
PHASE 2 — DESIGN SYSTEM AND LANGUAGE
Create a premium dark-theme editing interface with an optional light theme.

Default language: Kannada.
Secondary language: English.

All navigation items, tooltips, dialogs, errors, settings and buttons must support both languages.

Add a persistent language switch.

Create a responsive interface with:
- Left sidebar for projects and media.
- Central video preview.
- Multi-track timeline at the bottom.
- Right inspector panel for clip properties.
- Top toolbar for undo, redo, save, preview and export.
Use accessible controls, keyboard navigation and clear loading indicators.
PHASE 3 — DASHBOARD
Create functional dashboard sections for:
- New Project.
- Open Recent Project.
- Video Editor.
- Media Library.
- Audio Library.
- Titles and Subtitles.
- AI Script Assistant.
- Caption Editor.
- Thumbnail Maker.
- Export Queue.
- YouTube Settings.
- Instagram Settings.
- Facebook Settings.
- Application Settings.
- Storage and Backup.
Allow users to configure and edit their own YouTube, Instagram and Facebook profile URLs. Display these links in the top header and provide copy and open-link actions.

Never hardcode the user's account links.
PHASE 4 — PROJECT MANAGEMENT
Implement:
- Create, open, save, rename, duplicate and delete projects.
- Save projects with versioned JSON timeline documents.
- Store project metadata in SQLite in desktop mode.
- Store media files in a user-selected local folder.
- Allow projects to be reopened after application restart.
- Provide autosave, recovery and undo/redo.
- Track missing media files and allow users to relink them.
- Add project archive and restore.
- Add confirmation before destructive actions.
- Maintain a project-level asset manifest.
Never store large video binaries in SQLite or browser localStorage. Store media on disk and retain validated paths or file references in project metadata.

Do not delete the original source media when editing or exporting.
PHASE 5 — PROFESSIONAL MULTI-TRACK TIMELINE
Implement a real editable timeline with:
- Multiple video tracks.
- Multiple audio tracks.
- Image tracks.
- Text/title tracks.
- Subtitle tracks.
- Drag-and-drop clip positioning.
- Clip selection, move, trim, split and delete.
- Ripple delete and clip snapping.
- Timeline zoom and horizontal scrolling.
- Track visibility, locking, muting and solo controls.
- Playhead movement and frame-aware seeking.
- Clip duration and position editing.
- Undo and redo history.
- Keyboard shortcuts.
- Audio waveform display.
- Clip thumbnails where supported.
- Timeline serialization and restoration.
Each clip must have a stable ID, source reference, track ID, timeline start, source in-point, source out-point, duration and relevant effect properties.

Use a single, well-defined timebase. Handle overlapping clips, gaps, transitions and audio synchronization correctly.

Timeline actions must update the saved project model and preview. Do not implement fake drag-and-drop interactions.
PHASE 6 — VIDEO PREVIEW AND CLIP INSPECTOR
Implement:
- Play, pause, seek and frame stepping.
- Preview resolution settings.
- Playback speed controls.
- Clip transform controls: position, scale, crop and rotation.
- Opacity and volume controls.
- Brightness, contrast and saturation.
- Fade-in and fade-out.
- Clip speed adjustment.
- Text overlays.
- Image overlays.
- Basic transitions and effect parameters.
The preview must reflect the current timeline and clip properties. Use proxies or reduced-resolution previews where needed.

Handle unsupported codecs, damaged files and unavailable media with meaningful errors.
PHASE 7 — AUDIO AND SUBTITLES
Implement:
- Import audio files.
- Trim and split audio.
- Adjust gain and mute.
- Fade-in and fade-out.
- Multiple audio tracks.
- Import and export SRT subtitles.
- Create and edit subtitle segments.
- Adjust subtitle timing and duration.
- Choose supported fonts, font sizes, colours, outlines and positions.
- Support Kannada and English text rendering.
- Provide manual caption editing when automatic transcription is unavailable.
Keep audio and subtitle timing synchronized during export.
PHASE 8 — SOCIAL MEDIA PRESETS
Provide:
- YouTube landscape: 1920 × 1080.
- YouTube Shorts: 1080 × 1920.
- Instagram Reels: 1080 × 1920.
- Square video: 1080 × 1080.
- Facebook landscape: 1920 × 1080.
- Custom resolution and frame rate.
Allow users to switch aspect ratios and preview the crop before export.

Provide editable title, description, hashtag and thumbnail preparation tools for social media.

Publishing must remain a manual export/upload workflow unless a supported, authorized official API integration is configured.
PHASE 9 — REAL VIDEO EXPORT
Create a real export pipeline, not a simulated download.

Implement:
- MP4 export with H.264 video and AAC audio where supported.
- Resolution and frame-rate selection.
- Quality/bitrate settings.
- Audio inclusion and volume.
- Export progress and cancellation.
- Temporary-file cleanup.
- Error recovery and disk-space checks.
- Output folder selection.
- Export history.
- Validation of the finished output file.
Render the timeline using a real media-processing pipeline. Account for clip order, tracks, overlays, transitions, timing, transformations and audio mixing.

Use a suitable FFmpeg build in desktop mode. Use FFmpeg.wasm or another appropriate browser-compatible renderer for supported browser workflows.

If a feature cannot be rendered by the selected engine, disable it for that export or explain the limitation. Never silently omit an effect.

Verify export success by checking process exit status, output existence, file size and media metadata. Where available, run a playback or decoding validation test.
PHASE 10 — LOCAL-ONLY STORAGE AND PRIVACY
The default application must not upload user videos, audio, thumbnails or projects to a cloud storage service.

In desktop mode:
- Store project data in a local application data directory.
- Store SQLite database locally.
- Allow users to select media and output folders.
- Support portable project archives.
- Provide backup and restore to a user-selected folder.
- Do not send media or telemetry externally without explicit consent.
In browser mode:
- Explain browser storage limitations.
- Use IndexedDB and supported local file APIs.
- Ask users to reselect files when browser permissions or file references are lost.
- Do not claim browser data is permanently stored if it may be cleared by the browser.
Use a secure Electron configuration:
- contextIsolation enabled.
- nodeIntegration disabled.
- sandbox enabled where compatible.
- A narrow, validated preload API.
- Strict Content Security Policy.
- No arbitrary shell command execution from renderer input.
- Validate all IPC requests and file paths.
Do not expose filesystem access or unrestricted FFmpeg command execution to untrusted frontend input.
PHASE 11 — AI CREATOR TOOLS
Create optional AI modules for:
- Script writing and scene planning.
- Kannada and English caption assistance.
- Title and description suggestions.
- Thumbnail layout and text suggestions.
- Hashtag suggestions.
- Storyboard generation.
AI integrations must be modular and optional.

AI requests may require an external service and an API key. Never embed secret API keys in frontend code or distribute private keys inside the Windows application.

Use a secure API integration where needed, or provide local-model integration as a separate configurable option.

Explain exactly what data will leave the computer before any external AI request. Obtain user consent before sending text, media or transcripts externally.

Do not display generated captions, thumbnails or scripts as successful results until the relevant operation actually completes.

If AI is not configured, keep manual editing tools fully functional and show setup instructions.
PHASE 12 — ERROR HANDLING AND PERFORMANCE
Handle:
- Large video files.
- Insufficient RAM or disk space.
- Unsupported codecs.
- Corrupt or incomplete media.
- Interrupted rendering.
- Missing source files.
- Browser compatibility problems.
- Cancelled jobs.
- Permission errors.
- Failed project saves.
Use background workers or processes for expensive operations. Keep the UI responsive and show meaningful progress.

Implement safe cleanup of temporary files without deleting original media.
PHASE 13 — TESTING AND QUALITY ASSURANCE
Create automated tests for:
- Project creation and reopening.
- Timeline editing and undo/redo.
- Clip trimming and splitting.
- Multi-track synchronization.
- Subtitle timing.
- Project persistence.
- Missing-media relinking.
- Export cancellation and error handling.
- Social media presets.
- Kannada and English switching.
- Local-only storage behaviour.
- Electron security boundaries.
Create a reproducible sample project and short test media fixtures.

Run the build, automated tests and an end-to-end workflow from importing sample media to producing and validating an exported MP4.

Do not claim that tests passed unless they were actually executed. Report failed tests and remaining limitations honestly.
PHASE 14 — WINDOWS BUILD AND DOCUMENTATION
Provide:
- Complete source code and project folder structure.
- package.json scripts.
- Dependency installation instructions.
- Development launch instructions.
- Browser build instructions.
- Windows desktop development instructions.
- Windows installer build using a suitable packaging tool such as electron-builder.
- Configuration examples for optional AI services.
- Backup and restore instructions.
- Troubleshooting documentation.
- A feature-completion checklist.
The Windows installer must package the required application dependencies and a compatible FFmpeg binary where licensing and redistribution requirements permit.

Document FFmpeg and other third-party licenses. Do not bundle unlicensed codecs or claim that an unsigned installer is code-signed.
IMPLEMENTATION ORDER
Build in this order:
1. Project setup and application shell.
2. Local project and media management.
3. Preview player and timeline model.
4. Functional multi-track editing.
5. Audio, titles and subtitles.
6. Real video rendering and MP4 export.
7. Local persistence and recovery.
8. Kannada and English interface.
9. Social media presets and editable profile links.
10. Optional AI tools.
11. Automated testing and Windows installer.
After each phase, build and test the application before continuing.

Final requirement: deliver a usable video editor with real, tested core editing and export functionality. Provide exact commands to run it locally. Clearly label anything incomplete. Do not stop after creating the dashboard or a static interface.




